  "raw_meta_yaml": "schema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  name: fformat\n  version: \"1.0.3\"\n\npackage:\n  name: ${{ name }}\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\n# fpm fetches the dependencies (fregex, toml-f) during the build, at the tags in fpm.toml, as cargo\n# does for Rust packages; their licenses are included below\nsource:\n  url: https://github.com/jacobwilliams/fformat/archive/refs/tags/${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: 5766a437844232d0229ce7227ff13cca353c1145caa66d4c3ef87dac1e3f501e\n\nbuild:\n  number: 0\n  script: build.py\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    # gfortran: on Windows the MinGW one, as the fpm feedstock uses (the default Fortran compiler\n    # there is not gfortran); it is the one fformat's CI tests on Windows\n    - if: win\n      then:\n        - ${{ compiler('m2w64_fortran') }}\n        - ${{ stdlib('m2w64_c') }}\n      else:\n        - ${{ compiler('fortran') }}\n        - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n    # llvm-ar: the archiver on macOS (see build.py)\n    - if: osx\n      then: llvm-tools\n    - fpm >=0.13.0,<0.14\n    - git\n    - python >=3.9\n\ntests:\n  - files:\n      recipe:\n        - test/hello.f90\n    script:\n      # the executable reports the version of the package\n      - if: unix\n        then: test \"$(fformat --version)\" = \"fformat ${{ version }}\"\n        else: fformat --version | findstr /x /c:\"fformat ${{ version }}\"\n      - fformat --help\n      # a formatted file is left as it is\n      - fformat --check --no-config test/hello.f90\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://github.com/jacobwilliams/fformat\n  repository: https://github.com/jacobwilliams/fformat\n  documentation: https://jacobwilliams.github.io/fformat/\n  license: MIT\n  license_file:\n    - LICENSE\n    # the dependencies compiled into the executable\n    - build/dependencies/fregex/LICENSE\n    - build/dependencies/toml-f/LICENSE-MIT\n    - build/dependencies/toml-f/LICENSE-Apache\n  summary: A formatter for free-form Fortran, written in modern Fortran\n  description: |\n    fformat re-indents free-form Fortran (statements by nesting depth, continuation lines by\n    alignment), adjusts the blanks around operators, splits long lines, and can change the letter\n    case of keywords and the spelling of relational operators. The code tokens of the result are\n    checked against the input, so formatting never changes the code, and formatting a formatted\n    file changes nothing.\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - jacobwilliams\n",
